<script>
import createDealSource from 'crm/src/deals/createDeal.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import NewDeal from 'crm/src/deals/lib/NewDeal.abide'
import newDealSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/NewDeal.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import newDealTestSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/NewDeal.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A new deal is checked before it is written">
        <Example
            id="new-deal"
            app="crm"
            title={'`POST(fn, { args })` — a write checked before it runs'}
            summary="A new deal with no company and an amount of 0. Press Add deal: the server refuses it, with a message filed under each field that is wrong."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/NewDeal.abide': newDealSource,
                'src/deals/createDeal.handler.ts': createDealSource,
                'src/deals/lib/NewDeal.test.ts': newDealTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <NewDeal />
        </Example>

        <p><code>args</code> gates the arguments before the handler runs. A request that fails it
            is refused with 422 and a <code>ValidationError</code>, whose data is
            <code>Issues&lt;T&gt;</code>: the messages, keyed by the field's path.</p>

        <p>The form keeps the call. <code>saving = createDeal(…)</code> holds the value the call
            hands back, so <code>saving.busy()</code> is true while it is in flight and
            <code>saving.issues()</code> is the refusal's issues, or <code>undefined</code> when there
            are none. The next press replaces the call, and its answer replaces the last one's.
            <code>reactive&lt;typeof createDeal&gt;()</code> types the value as the call, so
            <code>saving.issues()?.company</code> is the company's messages.</p>

        <p><code>amount</code> starts as a number, so its field binds as one and the handler is
            handed a number.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the CRM</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>args</code></td><td>gates the arguments a handler is called with</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>schema</code></td><td>gates what a reactive value stores, on the side it lives on</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Schema&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>what either takes: a function returning what it accepts, or a Standard Schema such as zod's</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>jsonSchema</code></td><td>turns a JSON Schema document into a <code>Schema</code>, with its validator</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>JsonSchema</code></td><td>the document itself, published as written to OpenAPI and MCP</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>validateJson</code></td><td>checks a value against a document and hands back the issues, or <code>null</code>: a pasted row before an import</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Issues&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>the messages, keyed by path; a bare <code>string[]</code> on a primitive</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Paths&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>the keys of <code>Issues</code>: dot-joined, like <code>lines.0.amount</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>JsonValue</code></td><td>what an argument may hold, so it can travel and key a cache</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
